# Mobile env template.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Copy to `.env` — NOT `.env.local`. Expo loads these highest-priority first:
#   .env.<mode>.local  →  .env.local  →  .env.<mode>  →  .env
# (<mode> is NODE_ENV: `development` for `expo start`, `production` for builds
# and exports; `.env.local` is skipped entirely in `test` mode.)
#
# `.env.local` exists to override a *committed* `.env`. Nothing here is
# committed — the root .gitignore ignores every `.env*` at any depth — so with
# nothing shared to override, a second file buys only a precedence puzzle.
# One file. Use `.env`.
#
# Only `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` vars reach the client bundle. Metro inlines them by
# literal text match at build time, so `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`
# is substituted and `process.env[name]` is not — write names out in full.
# Anything behind the prefix ships to every device: never put a secret there.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE ONLY AFFECTS THE LOCAL METRO DEV SERVER.
#
# Real system env vars beat dotenv files, so EAS builds ignore it entirely
# (and it is gitignored, so it never uploads). Every key below must ALSO be
# set in eas.json under `build.preview.env` and `build.production.env`, or
# those builds ship pointing at nothing.
#
# The `development` and `development-simulator` profiles do NOT need them:
# both are developmentClient builds that load JS from Metro at runtime, and
# Metro reads this file. That is why those two profiles carry only
# EAS_BUILD_PROFILE.
#
# So: adding a new EXPO_PUBLIC_* key is a two-place edit — here, and in the
# preview + production blocks of eas.json.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase. Two ways to point this, and the default is the remote project.
#
# REMOTE (default). Point at the hosted dev project and leave it there. No
# containers to run, no context to swap when moving between this and the
# dashboard. Read both values from the project's API settings:
#   https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/_/settings/api
#
# LOCAL. Only if you want a throwaway database — testing a destructive
# migration, or working offline. Run `pnpm db:start`, then `pnpm db:status` to
# read the API URL and publishable key off the running containers:
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# Note `pnpm db:reset` wipes local data, and migrations still live in
# supabase/migrations either way — schema changes are authored there, never in
# the dashboard.
#
# Supabase's legacy `anon` / `service_role` JWTs are deprecated by the end of
# 2026. Use the publishable key (`sb_publishable_...`) here: it is designed to
# ship in a client bundle and, like `anon` before it, it does NOT bypass RLS.
# The matching `sb_secret_...` key DOES bypass RLS — it must never appear
# behind an EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix, or anywhere in this app at all.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# PostHog. Leave blank to disable analytics entirely — lib/analytics.ts builds
# no client without a key and every capture() call becomes a no-op, so local
# development does not need one.
#
# The project API key is write-only and safe to ship in the bundle; the
# personal API key (`phx_...`) is NOT and must never appear here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_API_KEY=
# Defaults to https://eu.i.posthog.com when unset — we are on PostHog EU Cloud
# deliberately, because it keeps event data in the EU and that is what our GDPR
# posture rests on. Do NOT point this at us.i.posthog.com: the project key is
# region-specific, so a US host would not just be the wrong jurisdiction, it
# would silently reject our events.
#
# Set only to override for a self-hosted instance.
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=
